{
  "note": "stack.json - the services this machine runs, as the supervisor starts them. The list is data: adding a world, changing a port or an argument is an edit here, not a program change. Start it with `cargo run -p kryta-supervisor` from the repository root; Ctrl+C stops every service in this list.",
  "information": {
    "what starts first": "nothing here is ordered: every service is started at once, and the supervisor waits for each one's READY line. The login program needs no other service; the world program reads its own databases at start-up.",
    "where the binaries come from": "target/debug/<program>.exe, so `cargo build` runs before the first start (the supervisor says which one is missing, and how to build it).",
    "what this file does not say": "the databases. They are checked by the programs themselves, never started here - a database server is an operating-system service, managed elsewhere.",
    "control_port": "the loopback port of the supervisor's control channel (`status`, `stop [service]`, `restart <service>`, `quit`). It is the only owner of these processes; the world's console asks the world to stop and exits with the reference's code 2 for a restart, and this program reads that code and starts the service again.",
    "console_service": "empty: no service inherits the terminal. A service's output is never shown under a supervisor, so an embedded console there would answer into nothing - run the service directly when you want its console, and use kryta-cli for a running world. Set this to a service name only if that service's output will not be hidden."
  },
  "control_port": 7612,
  "console_service": "",
  "services": [
    {
      "name": "auth",
      "program": "authserver",
      "args": [],
      "port": 6112
    },
    {
      "name": "world",
      "program": "worldserver",
      "args": [],
      "port": 6113
    },
    {
      "name": "manage",
      "program": "kryta-management-service",
      "args": ["-c", "tools/manage/manage.json"],
      "port": 6602
    }
  ]
}
